On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 23:57:57 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> I don't think it's true that all replies go to the first version? They
> go to the previous version or its cover letter, right?

git.git's documentation says the first version. I fetched it again
today. MyFirstContribution, in its overview of a thread:

  "Each iteration is sent with a new cover letter (like "[PATCH v2
  0/3]" above), itself a reply to the cover letter of the first
  iteration (more on that below)."

and in "Sending v2" with git send-email:

  "make sure to replace it with the correct Message-ID for your
  **first cover letter** - that is, for any subsequent version that
  you send, always use the Message-ID from v1."

git.git's .b4-config sets "send-same-thread = shallow". b4's config
documentation defines the values:

  yes: "B4 sends the first message of the new series as a reply to
  the previous version's cover letter."
  shallow: "B4 sends the first message of the new series as a reply
  to the first version's cover letter."

So git.git chose shallow over yes. SubmittingPatches itself only
says to send the versions "as replies to either an additional 'cover
letter' message (see below), the first patch, or the respective
preceding patch", and points to MyFirstContribution for the steps.

What you describe is real practice, though. My survey noted that
git contributors sometimes reply to the previous version instead, so
the chains deepen. It is b4's "yes", not git.git's rule.

> That way it produces a tree with a spine.

That is where I went wrong. The two rules give different shapes:

- First version (the patch, and git.git's rule): v2 to vN are all
  children of v1's cover, each with its patches and review below it.
  It is a fan, one level deep, not a spine.
- Previous version (b4's "yes"): v1, then v2 under it, v3 under v2,
  and so on. The versions form the spine, and each version's patches
  and review branch off it.

When you asked for the migration, you wrote that it should make
"trees with spines". I built and ran it as a fan, and did not point
out the difference. The migration now on mail@upstream, from
"[PATCH v2] libexec/mail: Add rethread.py" [1] and "[PATCH] mail:
Thread each revision under its first version" [2], has the same
fan shape, so it does not give you the spine either.

> Review the discussion on the list, and the git.git threading policy. I
> think you go this one wrong.

On the list, you chose "the git approach" in "Re: [RFC] threading
strategies for RFC and patch versions" [3], after my survey quoted
"always use the Message-ID from v1". The patch follows that quote. It
does not follow the spine you meant. There are two ways forward:

1. Keep the fan. It is git.git's documented rule, and nothing
   changes. The cost is that the versions of a series are siblings,
   not a chain. A reader sees them in date order under v1, but the
   thread does not show which version answered which review.
2. Switch to the spine. In format-patch.sh the walk up to v1 goes
   away, and --in-reply-to is simply v(N-1) from --previous, so patch
   1 gets smaller. rethread.py links each revision to v(N-1) instead
   of v1, and I run it on the list again, which touches about the
   same 123 files and needs one more notmuch rebuild on each host.
   The docs in patches 2 and 3 change to match. The cost is depth: a
   series with ten versions, like signoz, nests ten levels deep, which
   is what the kernel's rule and b4's "shallow" exist to avoid.

I recommend the spine, since it is the shape you asked for twice and
it records which version answers which review. Say which you want,
and the next pass sends v2 of "[PATCH 0/3] Thread each revision under
its first version" [4] and, for the spine, the second migration.

Separately, b/dev now carries "[PATCH] jails/instances/lab-mail: Spec
the list's mail hub" [5], so "[PATCH v2] jails/instances/lab-mail:
Spec the list's mail hub" [6] conflicted in my tree after the update.
I resolved it so that v2 sits on b/dev as a delta over v1. Its content
is unchanged; to take it, replace v1 with v2 in b/dev.

